{"batchcomplete":true,"query":{"pages":[{"pageid":3346025,"ns":0,"title":"Gary Null","revisions":[{"revid":1385401812,"parentid":1384017207,"minor":false,"timestamp":"2026-11-13T03:45:59Z","size":37887,"sha1":"12e9572df345f64157fa8ce1179b396731ce8d35","slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","content":"{{short description|American talk radio host and author who advocates for alternative medicine}}\n{{pp-sock|small=yes}}\n{{Use mdy dates|date=October 2019}}\n{{Infobox person\n| name               = Gary Null\n| image              = Gary Null Speaking Out Against Mandatory Vaccination in NYC.jpg\n| caption            = Null speaking against mandatory vaccinations in 2009\n| birth_date         = \n| birth_place        = [[Parkersburg]], [[West Virginia]], U.S.\n| occupation         = {{flatlist|\n* [[Radio personality|Talk radio host]]\n* [[Nutritionist]]\n* [[Author]]\n}}\n| known_for          = Production, promotion, and advocacy of pseudoscientific alternative medicine and medical [[quackery]]\n| education          = [[Thomas Edison State University]] <!--Use alma mater.-->\n| notable_works      = {{unbulleted list | ''The Gary Null Show''}}\n| spouse             = \n| children           = \n| signature          = \n}}\n'''Gary Michael Null''' is an American [[Radio personality|talk radio host]] and author who advocates pseudoscientific [[alternative medicine]] and produces a line of questionable [[dietary supplements]].\n\nNull is hostile to [[evidence-based medicine]] and has accused the medical community of being in [[Big Pharma conspiracy theory|a cabal with the pharmaceutical industry]] to suppress novel treatments for economic gains. He has promoted a range of [[pseudoscience|pseudo-scientific]] and ineffective alternative treatments, including ones for [[cancer]].<ref name=\"Butler-1992\" />\n\nHe is an [[HIV/AIDS denialism|HIV/AIDS denialist]]<ref name=\"salon\" /> who believes nutritional deficiencies are the causative agents of ''all'' illnesses, and has accordingly promoted fringe, diet-based treatment regimes for curing AIDS and other illnesses.<ref name=\"Butler-1992\" /> Null holds strong [[Anti-vaccine activism|anti-vaccination views]] and rejects the scientific consensus on topics such as [[water fluoridation]], [[genetically modified organisms]], and [[electromagnetic field]]s.<ref name=\"hall2010\" /><ref name=\"time\" /><ref>Fumento, Michael. (1996) ''Science Under Siege: How the Environmental Misinformation Campaign is Affecting Our Laws, Taxes, and Our Daily Lives'', W. Morrow, p. 285. {{ISBN|9780688147662}}.</ref><ref name=\"gorskisbm\" />\n\nReactions in the scholarly community to Null's claims have been generally negative, and Null along with his publications have been frequently criticized for disseminating misleading information that can negatively affect the public's understanding of health issues.\n\n==Biography==\n===Education===\nNull holds an [[Associate Degree|associate degree]] in [[business administration]] from the two-year, [[For-profit education|for-profit]] Mountain State College in [[Parkersburg, West Virginia]], and a [[bachelor's degree]] from [[Thomas Edison State College]] in human nutrition.<ref name=\"cred\">{{Cite web |url=http://www.cpt12.org/members/files/Gary_Null_Credentials_updated.pdf |title=Gary Null credentials |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20141211150929/http://www.cpt12.org/members/files/Gary_Null_Credentials_updated.pdf |archive-date=December 11, 2014 |access-date=October 18, 2019 }}</ref> He says he became interested in nutrition shortly after that while he was working as a part-time cook in [[New York City]].<ref name=\"Barrett_Null\"/> He later enrolled in a [[Ph.D.]] program in human nutrition and public health sciences from [[Union Institute & University]],<ref name=\"cred\" /> a private [[Distance education|distance-learning]] college headquartered in [[Cincinnati, Ohio]].<ref name=\"union-phd\">{{cite news | url=http://www.enquirer.com/editions/2004/03/27/loc_union27.html| work = [[Cincinnati Enquirer]] | title = Union Institute rules get stricter  | first = Kristina | last = Goetz | date = March 27, 2004 | access-date = June 23, 2011}}</ref> Null's doctoral thesis was entitled \"A Study of Psychological and Physiological Effects of Caffeine on Human Health\"; the degree was conferred in July 1989<ref name=\"healthrobbers\">Barrett, Stephen; Jarvis, William T. (1993) ''The Health Robbers: A Close Look at Quackery in America'', Prometheus Books. {{ISBN|9780879758554}}. pp 378-379.</ref> when he was 44 years old.\n\nNull's academic credentials were investigated by [[Stephen Barrett]], who expressed sharp skepticism about their quality and the quality of his PhD thesis.<ref name=\"Barrett_Null\">{{cite web | url=https://www.quackwatch.org/04ConsumerEducation/null.html | title=A Critical Look at Gary Null's Activities and Credentials | last=Barrett|first=Stephen | author-link=Stephen Barrett | date=January 29, 2012 | publisher=[[Quackwatch]] | access-date=December 29, 2019}}</ref> At the time of Null's education, Edison State College was a non-traditional institute that had no campus and conferred degrees via an external degree program, and towards which administrators evaluated \"college-level learning achieved through work or life experiences, self-study, college courses taken previously, industry-sponsored education programs, military instruction\" and other prior learning.<ref name=\"Barrett_Null\"/> Similarly, the rules for obtaining a PhD at Union Institute & University were a lot less rigid than typical academic practice, and allowed students to design their own course curriculum, form their doctoral committee, and attend only a few seminars; 13 years later, it would be subject to sanctions for failing to meet academic standards.<ref name=\"Barrett_Null\"/> Barrett said that the core member of the committee had no relevant subject expertise, having been chosen from the field of geology, and the other members (barring the peers) had contributed to Null's books or promoted alternative health supplements.<ref name=\"Barrett_Null\"/> Kurt Butler's 1992 book ''Consumer's Guide to Alternative Medicine'' raised similar questions and also reported that Null had long dodged queries about providing any relevant information (including precise time-spans) for his degrees.<ref name=\"Butler-1992\" />\n\n===Career===\nNull is hostile to many facets of mainstream medicine, arguing that physicians and pharmaceutical companies have an economic interest in promoting rather than preventing sickness, and he has regularly asserted that ''all'' diseases are caused by nutritional deficiencies which can be cured by nutritional supplements.<ref name=\"Butler-1992\">{{Cite book|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=vEMQAQAAMAAJ|title=A Consumer's Guide to \"alternative Medicine\": A Close Look at Homeopathy, Acupuncture, Faith-healing, and Other Unconventional Treatments|last=Butler|first=Kurt|date=1992|publisher=Prometheus Books|isbn=978-0-87975-733-5|pages=42–48|language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite book|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=4T5fDwAAQBAJ|title=The Skeptics' Guide to the Universe: How To Know What's Really Real in a World Increasingly Full of Fake|last=Novella|first=Steven|date=October 4, 2018|publisher=Hodder & Stoughton|isbn=978-1-4736-9641-9|pages=299|language=en|author-link=Steven Novella}}</ref> In place of standard medical therapy, Null advocated [[list of ineffective cancer treatments|alternative cancer treatments]] such as [[Krebiozen]], [[laetrile]] and [[Gerson therapy]], asserting that \"the alternatives have been covered up by those science writers of the national news media who ride shotgun for the medical establishment's solid-gold cancer train\".<ref name=\"Butler-1992\" /><ref name=\"nullfraud\">{{cite magazine|last=Null|first=Gary|author2=Robert Houston|year=1979|title=The Great Cancer Fraud|journal=[[Penthouse (magazine)|Penthouse]]|pages=76–78, 82, 268, 270, 272, 274, 276–278}}</ref><ref>{{cite magazine |last1=Kluger |first1=Jeffrey |title=The New Mister Natural |url=http://content.time.com/time/magazine/article/0,9171,24466-1,00.html |access-date=November 17, 2019 |magazine=[[Time (magazine)|Time]] |date=May 9, 1999 |archive-date=August 25, 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190825035714/http://content.time.com/time/magazine/article/0,9171,24466-1,00.html }}</ref> Null has also advocated for the long-debunked [[Revici's Guided Chemotherapy|Revici's chemotherapy]] in one of his radio-shows.<ref>{{Cite book|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=LsXmMHOT8VsC|title=Rational readings on environmental concerns|last=Herbert|first=Victor|date=1992|publisher=Van Nostrand Reinhold|isbn=0-442-01146-6|editor-last=Lehr|editor-first=Jay H.|pages=508|chapter=Unproven (Questionable) Dietary and Nutritional Methods in Cancer Prevention and Treatment|oclc=634225762}}</ref>\n\nOver the years, Null has owned multiple business ventures attempting to sell nutritional supplements for a wide range of diseases and disorders, along with a natural gourmet restaurant, a wellness retreat and an organic farm.<ref name=\":3\">{{Cite news|url=https://www.newspapers.com/image/472297972/|title=Healer or Hustler: Some call Gary Null the prophet of the alternative health movement. Others say he's just a profiteer.|last=Avenoso|first=Karen|date=January 24, 1994|work=[[New York Daily News]]|pages=37–38}}</ref>\n\nIn 1979–80, he co-authored a series of articles on cancer research for ''[[Penthouse (magazine)|Penthouse]]'', entitled \"The Politics of Cancer\", beginning with \"The Great Cancer Fraud\",<ref>Null's ''Penthouse'' articles on alternative cancer therapies include:\n*{{cite magazine|last=Null|first=Gary|author2=Robert Houston|year=1979|title=The Great Cancer Fraud|journal=[[Penthouse (magazine)|Penthouse]]|pages=76–78, 82, 268, 270, 272, 274, 276–278}}\n*{{cite magazine|last=Null|first=Gary|author2=A. Pitrone|year=1980|title=Suppression of new cancer therapies: Dr. Joseph Gold and hydrazine sulfate|journal=[[Penthouse (magazine)|Penthouse]]|pages=97–98, 160, 162–163}}\n*{{cite magazine|last=Null|first=Gary|author2=L. Steinman|year=1980|title=The politics of cancer. Part five. Suppression of new cancer therapies: Dr. Lawrence Burton|journal=[[Penthouse (magazine)|Penthouse]]|pages=75–76, 188–194, 196–197}}</ref> which opened: \"America's cancer plague has made the medical establishment and its media collaborators rich-even as they suppress new cancer cures\". They provided early coverage of the [[Burzynski Clinic]], a controversial clinic that offered an unproven cancer treatment, helping to bring it to public prominence,<ref name=\"SBM\">{{cite web|url=http://www.sciencebasedmedicine.org/stanislaw-burzynski-the-early-years-part-1/|title=Stanislaw Burzynski: The Early Years|author=Gorski, David|date=July 2, 2013|publisher=[[Science Based Medicine]]|access-date=February 20, 2014}}</ref><ref>Smith, Murray E. G. (1992). \"The Burzynski Controversy in the United States and in Canada: A Comparative Case Study in the Sociology of Alternative Medicine\", ''The Canadian Journal of Sociology'', 17:2 (Spring 1992). pp. 133-160.</ref> alleged that mainstream physicians advocate treatments that killed patients sooner than cancer itself and that conventional therapies amplified the disease.<ref>{{Cite thesis|last=Kelly|first=Matthew|title=The Sounds of Furious Living: Everyday Unorthodoxies in an Era of AIDS|date=2017|publisher=[[Columbia University]]|doi=10.7916/D8DJ5T1J}}</ref> In 1985, Null began writing a lengthy series of reports for ''Penthouse'' titled \"Medical Genocide\" that asserted mainstream medicine was completely ineffective in curing a range of major ailments from cardiac diseases to arthritis.<ref name=\"Butler-1992\"/> The series also promoted a range of nutrition regimens and alternative treatments for cancer including but not limited to [[laetrile]], [[krebiozen]], [[intermittent fasting]] and Gerson therapy as first-line therapy.<ref name=\"Butler-1992\" /> [[James Harvey Young]] described Null as a \"zealous journalist of unorthodoxy\", in the regard.<ref>{{Cite book|url=https://muse.jhu.edu/book/39037|title=The Medical Messiahs|last=Young|first=James Harvey|publisher=Princeton University Press|year=2015|isbn=978-1-4008-6869-8|pages=460|language=en|chapter=Afterword|chapter-url=https://muse.jhu.edu/chapter/1477826}}</ref><ref>{{Cite book|url=https://muse.jhu.edu/book/34160|title=American Health Quackery: Collected Essays of James Harvey Young|last=Young|first=James Harvey|date=2014|publisher=Princeton University Press|isbn=978-1-4008-6291-7|pages=43, 47|language=en|chapter=The foolmaster who fooled them|chapter-url=https://muse.jhu.edu/chapter/1292732}}</ref>\n\nNull is also an [[HIV/AIDS denialism|HIV/AIDS denialist]] and asserts the existence of government conspiracies to suppress effective diet-based treatments for AIDS.<ref name=\"Butler-1992\" /><ref>[[Nicoli Nattrass|Nattrass, Nicoli]]. \"[https://link.gale.com/apps/doc/A294895626/GPS?u=wikipedia&sid=GPS&xid=cc89f35f The social and symbolic power of AIDS denialism: AIDS denialism has proved socially resilient because dissident 'hero scientists' provide legitimacy, 'cultropreneurs' offer fake cures in the place of antiretroviral treatment, and HIV-positive 'living icons' seem to provide proof of concept].\" ''[[Skeptical Inquirer]]'', July-Aug. 2012, p. 34+. ''Gale In Context: Biography''. Accessed 19 Nov. 2019.</ref> As of 1999, his position was reported to be that the role that [[HIV]] played in [[AIDS]] was not as great as scientists generally believed, a discredited theory.<ref name=\"time\">{{cite magazine|url=http://content.time.com/time/magazine/article/0,9171,24466-1,00.html|title=The New Mister Natural|last=Park|first=Alice|date=May 17, 1999|magazine=[[Time (magazine)|Time]]|access-date=June 23, 2011|author2=Jeffrey Kluger|archive-date=August 25, 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190825035714/http://content.time.com/time/magazine/article/0,9171,24466-1,00.html|url-status=dead}}</ref> By 2013, however, Null was writing on his blog that \"HIV equals AIDS\" was a \"myth\".<ref>Null, Gary (March 7, 2013) [http://gna.squarespace.com/home/hiv-equals-aids-and-other-myths-of-the-aids-war-1.html \"HIV equals AIDS and Other Myths of the AIDS War\" ''The Gary Null Blog'']</ref> His book ''AIDS: A Second Opinion'' advocated for a range of dietary supplements for HIV-positive individuals instead of [[antiretroviral medication]].<ref name=\"salon\" /> Null also produced a variety of audio-visual media featuring other denialists, who spread misinformation about HIV tests and even alleged anti-retroviral therapy to be the causative agent of AIDS;<ref name=\":1\">{{Cite book|title=The AIDS Conspiracy: Science Fights Back|last=Nattrass|first=Nicoli|date=2012|publisher=Columbia University Press|isbn=978-0-231-52025-6|pages=[https://archive.org/details/aidsconspiracysc0000natt/page/116 116–118, 128]|chapter=HERO SCIENTISTS, CULTROPRENEURS, LIVING ICONS, AND PRAISE-SINGERS: AIDS DENIALISM AS COMMUNITY|jstor=10.7312/natt14912|doi=10.7312/natt14912|chapter-url=https://archive.org/details/aidsconspiracysc0000natt/page/116}}</ref> the [[OPV AIDS hypothesis]] was propounded for the first time over one of his radio-shows, by a fellow foot-soldier.<ref name=\":2\">{{Cite book|url=https://muse.jhu.edu/book/16005|title=Vaccine: The Debate in Modern America|last=Largent|first=Mark A.|date=2012|publisher=Johns Hopkins University Press|isbn=978-1-4214-0672-5|pages=52, 59, 64|language=en|chapter=Sources of Doubt|chapter-url=https://muse.jhu.edu/chapter/543430}}</ref><ref>Katrak, Sarosh. \"[https://link.gale.com/apps/doc/A144057827/GPS?u=wikipedia&sid=GPS&xid=1a3f22ea The origin of HIV and AIDS: An enigma of evolution].\" ''Annals of Indian Academy of Neurology'', vol. 9, no. 1, 2006. ''Gale General OneFile''. Accessed 19 Nov. 2019.</ref> Some of Null's productions portrayed those patients as the real heroes, who rejected anti-retroviral therapy in favor of his nutrition-based regimen.<ref name=\":1\" /> Null's articles (and alternative treatment regimens) have been featured over the website of [[Peter Duesberg]].<ref name=\":1\" />\n\nIn 1999, ''[[Time (magazine)|Time]]'' magazine wrote of Null: \"From a young reporter this is to be expected. But two decades later, Null, 54, is still warning of a variety of medical bogeymen out to gull a trusting public\";<ref name=\"time\" /> other sources have reported Null's view that HIV does not cause [[HIV/AIDS|AIDS]].<ref name=\"salon\" /><ref>Nattrass, Nicoli and Kalichman, Seth C. [https://books.google.com/books?id=7r4TbLuyFZEC&dq=%22Gary+Null%22+HIV%2FAIDS+denial&pg=PA124 \"The Politics and Psychology of AIDS Denialism\"] in Rohleder, Poul; Swartz Leslie; Kalichman, Seth C.; Simbaya, Leickness Chisamu eds. (2009) ''1HIV/AIDS in South Africa 25 Years On: Psychosocial Perspectives''. Springer. p.124. {{isbn|9781441903068}}</ref> ''[[Salon (website)|Salon]]'' described his work as \"massive, irresponsible and nearly unreadable\".<ref name=\"salon\">{{cite web | work = [[Salon (website)|Salon]] | first = Peter | last = Kurth | title = Quack record | date = May 21, 2002 | access-date = June 23, 2011 | url = https://www.salon.com/2002/05/21/null/| url-status = live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20021002024057/http://www.salon.com/books/feature/2002/05/21/null/index.html|archive-date=October 2, 2002}}</ref> AIDS advocacy groups have asked for his works to be censured, as detrimental to public health.<ref name=\":1\" /> [[Seth Kalichman]], professor of social psychology at the [[University of Connecticut]], has decried Null's role as a prominent proponent of [[AIDS denialism]] and has accused him of cashing in on HIV/AIDS. In his 2009 book called ''Denying AIDS'', he compared Null's activities to [[Holocaust denial]] and described Null as an example of a dangerous entrepreneur who \"obviously breached\" the balance between [[free speech]] and protecting [[public health]].<ref name=\"denying-aids\">{{cite book|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=_mtDBCDwxugC|title=Denying AIDS: Conspiracy Theories, Pseudoscience, and Human Tragedy|last=Kalichman|first=Seth C.|publisher=[[Springer Science+Business Media|Springer]]|year=2009|isbn=978-0-387-79475-4|pages=12, 89|author-link=Seth Kalichman}}</ref> [[Nicoli Nattrass]] described Null as a 'cultopreneur'.<ref name=\":1\" />\n\nIn 2010, Null reported that he became ill and had to see his doctor and that six other consumers were hospitalized for [[Hypervitaminosis D|vitamin D poisoning]] after ingesting a nutritional supplement manufactured by his own contractor. In a lawsuit against the company, he alleged that the supplement erroneously contained more than 1,000 times the dose of [[vitamin D]] reported on the label. Null received numerous telephone calls from customers while himself in severe pain.<ref name=\"abc-news\">{{cite news | work = [[ABC News (United States)|ABC News]] | title = Alternative Health Guru Sues Company Over His Own Product | first = Cleopatra | last = Andreadis | date = April 29, 2010 | access-date = March 30, 2019| url = https://abcnews.go.com/TheLaw/Health/alternative-health-guru-sues-company-product-claiming-killed/story?id=10499409}}</ref> The ''[[Los Angeles Times]]'' wrote that Null's experience \"should give pause to anyone lured by the extravagant claims of many supplement makers\", and said that it was common for dietary supplements to contain doses \"wildly different than those indicated on their label\" as a result of weak [[Dietary supplement#Regulation|regulation]].<ref name=\"la-times\">{{cite news | work = [[Los Angeles Times]] | title = Supplements guru sues over his own product | url = http://latimesblogs.latimes.com/booster_shots/2010/04/supplements-guru-sues-over-his-own-product.html | date = April 29, 2010 | access-date = July 15, 2014 | first = Melissa | last = Healy |url-access=subscription }}</ref>\n\nNull had been a keynote speaker at a rally opposing mandatory [[H1N1 influenza]] vaccination during the [[2009 flu pandemic]], leading the [[New York State Department of Health]] to hold a simultaneous conference to dismiss Null's claims about the vaccine as \"not scientifically credible\" by discussing the [[clinical trials]].<ref name=\"h1n1-vaccine\">{{cite news|url=http://www.legislativegazette.com/Articles-c-2009-11-02-55041.113122-Health-workers-angry-over-mandatory-swine-flu-shots.html|title=Health workers angry over mandatory swine flu shots|last=Scribona|first=Charles|date=November 2, 2009|work=Legislative Gazette|access-date=June 23, 2011|archive-date=July 24, 2013|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130724172942/http://www.legislativegazette.com/Articles-c-2009-11-02-55041.113122-Health-workers-angry-over-mandatory-swine-flu-shots.html}}</ref> Null had opposed public vaccination deeming them as unsafe and ineffective treatments; he has also promoted discredited notions of vaccines causing [[Vaccines and autism|autism]] and other ailments, including leading to infant death.<ref name=\":4\" /><ref name=\"Butler-1992\" /><ref name=\":2\" /><ref>Laskowski, Marysia. \"[https://search.ebscohost.com/login.aspx?direct=true&db=a9h&AN=113422220&site=eds-live Nudging Towards Vaccination: A Behavioral Law and Economics Approach to Childhood Immunization Policy].\" ''Texas Law Review'', vol. 94, no. 3, Feb. 2016, pp. 601–628. ''EBSCOhost''.</ref> Discussing Null's anti-vaccination efforts, [[Harriet Hall]] deems Null to have a bad track record for scientific credibility.<ref name=\"hall2010\">[[Harriet Hall|Hall, Harriet]]. \"[https://link.gale.com/apps/doc/A220057904/GPS?u=wikipedia&sid=GPS&xid=dfe821fb Swine flu vaccine fearmongering].\" ''[[Skeptic (U.S. magazine)|Skeptic]]'' [Altadena, CA], vol. 15, no. 3, 2010, p. 16+. ''Gale In Context: Biography''. Accessed 19 Nov. 2019.</ref>\n\nJonathan Howard, former director of Neurology department at [[Bellevue Hospital]], stated that Null's book ''Death by Medicine'' (wherein he had calculated conventional medicine to be the single-largest cause of death in America), was statistically flawed and ill-intended, with an aim to gain on a potential rift between patients and mainstream physicians.<ref>{{Cite book|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=8IF8DwAAQBAJ|title=Cognitive Errors and Diagnostic Mistakes: A Case-Based Guide to Critical Thinking in Medicine|last=Howard|first=Jonathan|date=November 28, 2018|publisher=Springer|isbn=978-3-319-93224-8|pages=11|language=en}}</ref>\n\nNull has been also a supporter of [[Therapeutic touch|touch therapy]] and [[magnet therapy]], both of which have been long determined to not provide any tangible health benefits.<ref name=\":0\">{{Cite book|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=H9f5f2th264C|title=Scary Monsters and Bright Ideas|last=Williams|first=Robyn|publisher=UNSW Press|year=2000|isbn=978-0-86840-707-4|pages=38|language=en}}</ref><ref name=\"Rosa 1005–1010\">{{cite journal|last1=Rosa|first1=Linda|last2=Rosa|first2=E|author-link2=Emily Rosa|last3=Sarner|first3=L|last4=Barrett|first4=S|author-link4=Stephen Barrett|date=April 1, 1998|title=A Close Look at Therapeutic Touch|journal=JAMA|volume=279|issue=13|pages=1005–1010|doi=10.1001/jama.279.13.1005|pmid=9533499}}</ref><ref name=\"ACS\">{{cite book|title=American Cancer Society Complete Guide to Complementary and Alternative Cancer Therapies|publisher=[[American Cancer Society]]|year=2009|isbn=978-0-944235-71-3|editor=Ades TB|edition=2nd|pages=[https://archive.org/details/americancancerso0000unse/page/248 248–251]|chapter=Therapeutic Touch|chapter-url=https://archive.org/details/americancancerso0000unse/page/248}}</ref><ref name=\"TrickOrTreatment\">{{Cite book|title=Trick or Treatment|title-link=Trick or Treatment|author9=Singh, S. and Ernst, E.|publisher=Corgi|year=2008|pages=267–268}}</ref><ref name=\"cochrane-acute\">{{cite journal|last=O'Mathúna|first=DP|editor1-first=Dónal P|editor1-last=O'Mathúna|date=May 3, 2016|title=Therapeutic touch for healing acute wounds|journal=Cochrane Database Syst Rev|type=Systematic review|volume=5|issue=5|article-number=CD002766|doi=10.1002/14651858.CD002766.pub4|pmid=27140910}}</ref><ref name=\"acsMag\">{{cite book|title=American Cancer Society Complete Guide to Complementary & Alternative Cancer Therapies|date=2009|publisher=American Cancer Society|isbn=978-0-944235-71-3|editor1-last=Russell|editor1-first=Jill|edition=2nd|location=Atlanta, Ga.|chapter=Magnetic therapy|editor2-last=Rovere|editor2-first=Amy|url-access=registration|url=https://archive.org/details/americancancerso0000unse}} See archived online version {{cite web|url=http://www.cancer.org/Treatment/TreatmentsandSideEffects/ComplementaryandAlternativeMedicine/HerbsVitaminsandMinerals/magnetic-therapy|title=Magnetic Therapy|date=November 1, 2008|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20121112145357/http://www.cancer.org/Treatment/TreatmentsandSideEffects/ComplementaryandAlternativeMedicine/HerbsVitaminsandMinerals/magnetic-therapy|archive-date=November 12, 2012}}  [[Electromagnetic therapy]] is a related field.  See chapter in ACS book just referenced, and [https://web.archive.org/web/20121210093642/http://www.cancer.org/Treatment/TreatmentsandSideEffects/ComplementaryandAlternativeMedicine/ManualHealingandPhysicalTouch/electromagnetic-therapy archived ACS webpage] on that.</ref><ref name=\"Park_Voodoo\">{{cite book|title=Voodoo Science: The Road from Foolishness to Fraud|last=Park|first=Robert L.|publisher=Oxford University Press|year=2000|isbn=0-19-513515-6|location=New York, New York|pages=[https://archive.org/details/voodooscienceroa00park/page/58 58–63]|quote=Not only are magnetic fields of no value in healing, you might characterize these as \"homeopathic\" magnetic fields.|author-link=Robert L. Park|url=https://archive.org/details/voodooscienceroa00park/page/58}}</ref> In a product brochure, he falsely claimed of magnets being inserted in space suits to avoid adverse complications in astronauts.<ref>[[Robert L. Park|Park, Robert L.]] \"[https://link.gale.com/apps/doc/A66496155/GPS?u=wikipedia&sid=GPS&xid=7b68cc8c Three More Little Adventures on the Voodoo Science Front].\" ''[[Skeptical Inquirer]]'', Nov. 2000, p. 6. ''Gale In Context: Biography''. Accessed 19 Nov. 2019.</ref> He has also promoted [[homeopathy]], vouched for [[pangamic acid]] to be Vitamin B15.<ref name=\":4\">{{Cite web|url=https://sciencebasedmedicine.org/gary-nulls-attack-on-sbm/|title=Gary Null's Attack on SBM|last=Novella|first=Steven|author-link=Steven Novella|website=SBM|date=August 7, 2019|language=en-US|access-date=October 16, 2019}}</ref><ref name=\"Butler-1992\" />\n\nButler referred to Null's very many fringe assertions in the field of nutrition spanning from claims that fatty meats are difficult to digest, that meats do not provide any energy and milk is not a good source of calcium, to the claim Vitamin C increases body requirements for iron and certain nutrients are preferable to be consumed in daytime, while the rest in night-time.<ref name=\"Butler-1992\" /><ref>{{Cite journal|last=Davis|first=Alan|date=Spring 2001|title=Not Responsible for Items Forgotten or Lost|url=https://www.questia.com/read/1P3-3584745181/not-responsible-for-items-forgotten-or-lost|journal=[[The Hudson Review]]|volume=54|issue=1|pages=141–147|doi=10.2307/3852833|jstor=3852833|via=[[Questia]]|url-access=subscription}}{{dead link|date=October 2026|bot=medic}}{{cbignore|bot=medic}}</ref> Null also recommends [[coffee enema]]s and advocates for [[Craniosacral therapy|cranial osteopathy]], [[applied kinesiology]] and [[pulsed electromagnetic field therapy]].<ref name=\"Butler-1992\" /> [[Corby Kummer]] noted Null's ''Vegetarian Handbook'' to contain an outlandish combination of plant foods supposedly high in protein.<ref>[[Corby Kummer|Kummer, Corby]]. \"[https://link.gale.com/apps/doc/A201491084/GPS?u=wikipedia&sid=GPS&xid=743f9848 What vegetarians don't get: the answer isn't what you think, and it can be a problem for meat-eaters, too].\" ''[[The Atlantic]]'', June 1991, p. 106+. ''Gale In Context: Biography''. Accessed 19 Nov. 2019.</ref>\n\nNull has produced many works (incl. television programs and books) about reversing aging; he rejects mainstream scholarship deeming the inevitable progression of senescence as normalcy and instead typifies a popular mis-construal about the aging body being an abnormal deviant.<ref>{{Cite book|chapter-url=https://dspace.sunyconnect.suny.edu/bitstream/handle/1951/544/0791462315.pdf|title=Amending the abject body: aesthetic makeovers in medicine and culture|last=Covino|first=Deborah Caslav|date=2004|publisher=State University of New York Press|isbn=978-0-7914-6231-7|location=New York|page=101|language=en|chapter=Making over Abjection|oclc=897112826}}</ref>\n\n''[[Science-Based Medicine]]'' described Null as a consistent opponent of [[evidence-based medicine]].<ref name=\"gorskisbm\">{{Cite web|url=https://sciencebasedmedicine.org/the-null-hypothesis-gary-null-attacks-science-based-medicine/|title=The Null hypothesis: Gary Null attacks science-based medicine|last=Gorski|first=David|author-link=David Gorski|website=SBM|date=May 14, 2018|language=en-US|access-date=October 16, 2019}}</ref> Butler said Null was the foremost promoter of dangerous health-related misinformation to the public and sarcastically remarked that Null is so often wrong, it may be better for an average audience to believe the precise opposite of what he says.<ref name=\"Butler-1992\" />\n\nNull has been a popular author and commands a large following.<ref name=\":3\" /> He has been criticised by fellow practitioners of alternative medicine including [[Andrew Weil]], [[People With AIDS]].<ref name=\":3\" /> He has been frequently published over ''[[Townsend Letter]]'', a periodical focusing on [[alternative medicine]].<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://link.galegroup.com/apps/doc//GPS?sid=lms|title=Gale Power Search - Results - Author (\"Gary null\") And Publication Title (Townsend Letter for Doctors And Patients)|website=link.galegroup.com|language=en|access-date=2019-11-19}}{{Dead link|date=March 2023 |bot=InternetArchiveBot |fix-attempted=yes }}</ref>\n\n==Media work==\n\nNull began broadcasting a syndicated radio talk show, ''Natural Living with Gary Null'', in 1980. His show was broadcast first on [[WBAI]], then on the VoiceAmerica Network and over the Internet. Null's show subsequently returned to WBAI, leading to protests from [[AIDS Coalition to Unleash Power|ACT-UP New York]] and other AIDS activist groups concerned by Null's promotion of AIDS denialism.<ref name=\"act-up\">{{cite web | publisher = [[AIDS Coalition to Unleash Power|ACT-UP]] | title = Letter to Indra Hardat, interim general manager, WBAI/Pacifica | url = http://www.actupny.org/reports/denialist_gary_null.html | date = January 17, 2006 | access-date = June 23, 2011}}</ref><ref name=\"aidstruth-wbai\">{{cite web | publisher = aidstruth.org | title = WBAI: Do not put Gary Null's dangerous show on the air | url = http://www.aidstruth.org/news/2010/wbai-do-not-put-gary-nulls-dangerous-show-air-sign | date = November 17, 2010 | access-date = June 23, 2011 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20110811063012/http://aidstruth.org/news/2010/wbai-do-not-put-gary-nulls-dangerous-show-air-sign | archive-date = August 11, 2011 }}</ref> He continues to host ''The Gary Null Show'' through the Progressive Radio Network, which he established in 2005. His shows attracted about a fifth of the total audience-subscriptions to WBAI circa 1994<ref name=\":3\" /> and he was speculated to have incurred the maximum revenues, in the history of the WBAI station, even during its brief shutdown in October 2019.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.ny1.com/nyc/brooklyn/politics/2019/10/07/wbai-shuts-its-door-progressive-politics-radio-station-new-york-city|title=End of an Era: Radical Station WBAI Shuts its Doors|website=www.ny1.com|language=en|access-date=October 17, 2019}}</ref> Butler has written that Null has provided potentially dangerous and outright dubious medical advice to a variety of patient-callers via these fora.<ref name=\"Butler-1992\" />\n\n=== PBS ===\n\nNull has made several self-funded and self-produced documentary films on [[public policy]], personal health, and development. These have been aired by [[PBS]] during pledge drives, leading to a surge in sales of his books.<ref name=Variety>{{cite news|last=Katz|first=Richard|url=https://variety.com/1998/biz/news/null-zeroes-in-on-pbs-fills-void-in-coffers-1117489714/|title=Null zeroes in on PBS, fills void in coffers|work=[[Variety (magazine)|Variety]]|date=December 23, 1998|access-date=September 29, 2013}}</ref><ref name=BookSales>{{cite news|last=Quinn|first=Judy|url=http://www.publishersweekly.com/pw/print/19990412/35096-gary-null-s-book-sales-get-healthier.html|title=Gary Null's Book Sales Get Healthier|work=[[Publishers Weekly]]|date=April 12, 1999|access-date=September 29, 2013}}</ref> The use of Null's films in PBS pledge drives has raised ethical concerns for those involved with the network, who felt that Null's claims were pseudo-scientific and that PBS should not promote them.<ref name=\"Farhi\">{{cite news|last=Farhi|first=Paul|url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/archive/politics/1998/12/26/pbss-new-age-answer-to-age-old-problem/97d190c8-1208-4010-8b16-687afef98c38/|title=PBS'S New-Age Answer to Age-Old Problem|newspaper=The Washington Post|date=December 26, 1998|access-date=March 30, 2019}}</ref><ref name=\":0\" /><ref>{{Cite book|title=NPR: The Trials and Triumphs of National Public Radio|last=McCauley|first=Michael P.|date=2005|publisher=Columbia University Press|isbn=978-0-231-50995-4|pages=109|chapter=A Civilized Voice in a New Media Environment|jstor=10.7312/mcca12160|doi=10.7312/mcca12160}}</ref> While Null's films were highly<ref name=\":0\" /> effective in generating financial contributions, the president of PBS, [[Ervin Duggan]], expressed concern that such programming \"open[ed] the door to quacks and charlatans\".<ref>{{cite web | last = Bedford | first = Karen Everhart | title = Gary Null special sparks debate on pledge program standards | work = [[Current (newspaper)|Current]] | date = January 25, 1999 | url = http://www.current.org/mo/mo901n.html | access-date = January 19, 2009 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20090306011355/http://www.current.org/mo/mo901n.html | archive-date = March 6, 2009 }}</ref> [[KCTS-TV|Some member stations]] have refused to broadcast his programs.<ref name=\"Farhi\" />\n\n''[[Discover (magazine)|Discover]]'' magazine's [[Keith Kloor]] condemned Null's 2012 documentary film ''Seeds of Death: Unveiling the Lies of GMOs'', writing that the film:\n{{blockquote|... is a classic collection of all the untruths, myths, and tropes commonly used by the anti-GMO movement. The scope of its dishonesty is brazen... This is crazy train stuff said with a straight face. The worldview that allows someone to believe such things cannot be penetrated with legitimate scientific information.<ref name=\"Kloor Keith\">{{cite news|url=http://blogs.discovermagazine.com/collideascape/2013/05/31/gary-null-cultivator-of-dangerous-woo-plants-seeds-of-death/#.VcNPlJ1VhBd|title=Gary Null, Cultivator of Dangerous Woo, Plants Seeds of Death|last=Kloor Keith|author-link=Keith Kloor|date=31 May 2013|work=Collide-a-Scape|publisher=[[Discover (magazine)|Discover]]|access-date=6 August 2015|archive-date=September 5, 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150905052211/http://blogs.discovermagazine.com/collideascape/2013/05/31/gary-null-cultivator-of-dangerous-woo-plants-seeds-of-death/#.VcNPlJ1VhBd}}</ref>}}\n\n===Film===\n\nNull has written, directed and self-produced dozens of documentary-style films. ''[[Poverty Inc. (Gary Null film)|Poverty Inc]]'' was released in 2014 to poor reviews from critics.<ref name=\"LATimes-PovertyInc\">{{cite news | url=http://www.latimes.com/entertainment/movies/la-et-mn-poverty-inc-review-20141205-story.html | title='Poverty Inc.' gives long lecture on the banking world | work=Los Angeles Times | date=December 4, 2014 | access-date=August 14, 2015}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|last=Gates|first=Anita|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2014/12/05/movies/poverty-inc-paints-a-bleak-picture-of-life-after-2008.html|title=The Corporate Creation of a New Class Structure|work=The New York Times|date=December 4, 2014|access-date=March 30, 2019}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.villagevoice.com/2014/12/03/doc-poverty-inc-fails-to-inform-or-guide/|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180515183642/https://www.villagevoice.com/2014/12/03/doc-poverty-inc-fails-to-inform-or-guide/|url-status=dead|archive-date=May 15, 2018|title=Doc Poverty Inc. Fails to Inform or Guide|website=www.villagevoice.com|date=December 3, 2014|access-date=October 18, 2019}}</ref> Other films include ''Autism: Made in the U.S.A.'' (2009)<ref>Variety [https://variety.com/2009/film/reviews/autism-made-in-the-u-s-a-1200474869 Review: 'Autism: Made in the U.S.A.'; Andrew Barker]</ref> and ''Gulf War Syndrome: Killing Our Own'' (2007).<ref>[[Miami New Times]] [http://www.miaminewtimes.com/movies/gulf-war-syndrome-killing-our-own-6616573 Gulf War Syndrome: Killing Our Own (NR)] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160305235323/http://www.miaminewtimes.com/movies/gulf-war-syndrome-killing-our-own-6616573 |date=March 5, 2016 }}</ref>\n\n== References ==\n{{reflist}}\n\n== External links ==\n* {{IMDb name|1567037}}\n\n{{Authority control}}\n\n{{DEFAULTSORT:Null, Gary}}\n[[Category:1945 births]]\n[[Category:Living people]]\n[[Category:American anti-vaccination activists]]\n[[Category:American conspiracy theorists]]\n[[Category:American documentary filmmakers]]\n[[Category:American health activists]]\n[[Category:American health and wellness writers]]\n[[Category:American nutritionists]]\n[[Category:American talk radio hosts]]\n[[Category:American self-help writers]]\n[[Category:Anti-GMO activists]]\n[[Category:Businesspeople from Parkersburg, West Virginia]]\n[[Category:HIV/AIDS denialists]]\n[[Category:Penthouse (magazine) people]]\n[[Category:People from Parkersburg, West Virginia]]\n[[Category:Pseudoscientific diet advocates]]\n[[Category:Thomas Edison State University alumni]]\n[[Category:Union Institute & University alumni]]"}}}],"contentmodel":"wikitext","pagelanguage":"en","pagelanguagehtmlcode":"en","pagelanguagedir":"ltr","touched":"2026-11-17T19:29:22Z","lastrevid":1385401812,"length":37887}]}}